# Companion/control case for kexhq/kex#343's fix
# (declared_function_shadows_ambiguous_import.kex): with no program-declared
# `parse` to shadow it, the collision is still reported ambiguous. The fix
# must only skip the ambiguity check when a declaration actually shadows it,
# never suppress the diagnostic outright.
#
# `Date.parse`, `DateTime.parse`, and `Integer.parse` (all String ->
# Result<_, _>) collide; the two named in the message are always the
# lexicographically smallest pair of the modules that actually do, a pure
# function of which modules collide rather than of iteration order over a
# hash map (the report used to name whichever pair a nested loop over that
# map happened to meet first, which differed by platform and could silently
# miss a third collider entirely — this file used to expect 'Kex.DateTime'
# and 'Kex.Integer' until CI's wasm build, whose prelude interface is built
# from source rather than a prebuilt registry, showed 'Kex.Date' colliding
# too).
main do
  IO.printLine(parse("x"))
end
